<h1>⭐ Star Stones</h1>
<div class="collection-intro">
<p class="lead"><strong>Asterism</strong> is a star of light that seems to float on the surface of a domed (cabochon) gem. It moves as you tilt the stone or move the light.</p>
</div>
<div class="formation-grid">
  <div><h3>Needle inclusions</h3><p>Microscopic needles, usually <strong>rutile</strong> (titanium oxide), grow in the crystal in neat, parallel sets. In some stones the needles are hematite, ilmenite or magnetite. Each set reflects light as a bright line at right angles to the needles.</p></div>
  <div><h3>Crossing rays</h3><p>Sets of needles in different directions make the lines cross. In hexagonal crystals like corundum and quartz, three sets give a <strong>6-rayed</strong> star. Two sets give a <strong>4-rayed</strong> star, as in diopside, most Idaho garnet and moonstone.</p></div>
  <div><h3>12-ray stars</h3><p>When two different kinds of inclusions overlap, a stone can show <strong>12 rays</strong>. This happens, for example, in some Thai black star sapphires, where rutile and hematite needles sit at offset angles.</p></div>
  <div><h3>Cut matters</h3><p>The star appears only when the stone is cut as a smooth dome with its base parallel to the needles. Strong single light (sunlight or a penlight) shows it best. Synthetic and diffusion-treated star stones exist, and their stars often look unnaturally sharp and perfect.</p></div>
</div>
